Josh Rambo wrote:
> sjdevnull@yahoo.com wrote:
>> Certainly you're not going to come up with a system that measures the
>> elegance or skill with which a game was played, but that doesn't mean
>> that the current system can't be improved or that progress through the
>> game is completely impossible to measure.  
> 
> I think the point he was trying to make wasn't that we should make a new
> scoring system. I think it was that there's no possible way to
> programatically access how well someone is playing. As you've pointed
> out yourself, people play differently. Making a scoring method that
> wouldn't give advantage to one style of play over another is impossible,
> as far as I can see. I think that's more or less what he was saying.

Pish, tosh.  Nothing should ever be said to be impossible.

There are many tools available in NH which would be suitable to measure
score.  Turncount.  Real-time.  Conducts.  Use/lack thereof of clever
trickery (did you alchemize?).  And so on.

Currently, the method of measuring score is not particularly
representative of player skill, as witnessed by the two max-score
ascensions on NAO, as well as the multiple gazillion-digit ascensions
immediately following.

However, that does not mean that it would be possible to collect a
number of players who could be said to be "highly skilled" at the game
and yet have different playstyles, examine which things are common to
all, and alter the scoring system accordingly... along with
corresponding lesser scores for ineffective use of same.  (blowing up
potions via careless alchemy with cursed ones, for example.  Engraving
Elbereth while confused.  These are only examples; final real points of
measure might be entirely different.)

The more accurate you wish to make your scoring system, by necessity the
more complex it will be.  But you could very definitely come up with
something that was more accurate than the current one, which is, well,
primarily only accurate for people who can't ascend.

